cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this replication package, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Calibrating M-type unit-root tests in finite samples: exact tables, the sieve bootstrap and a kernel long-run variance"
version: "1.0.1"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23424031"
authors:
  - family-names: "Téllez Crespo"
    given-names: "Andrés Felipe"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-7680-6585"
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher, Mexico City"
repository-code: "https://github.com/andrestellez84/m-unit-root-calibration"
# MIT for the code; CC BY 4.0 for the results, the supplement, the figures and the documentation (see README.md, License)
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  The M unit-root tests of Ng and Perron are usually applied with asymptotic
  critical values and an autoregressive estimate of the long-run variance. We
  examine both choices at exact size. Because the statistics are scale
  invariant, their null law under i.i.d. Gaussian errors can be simulated
  exactly at the sample size in hand. The GLS estimate of the mean biases the
  second moment of the detrended series by a term of order 1/(n + c-bar^2)
  with a large constant, and the published asymptotic critical values of
  MSB-GLS reject 0.026 at n = 200 in the trend case. This correction is
  specific to the i.i.d. null. Under serially correlated errors the simulated
  critical values of MZt-GLS are on average no closer to the nominal level than
  the asymptotic ones, while a sieve bootstrap lowers the mean absolute size
  distortion over thirty designs from 0.0135 to 0.0074, at a cost of up to 4.2
  points of size-adjusted power. Replacing the autoregressive estimate by a
  Bartlett estimate with an Andrews bandwidth adds power under i.i.d. errors
  but loses size control under serial correlation, rejecting 0.42 at n = 200
  with moving-average errors of -0.8; fixed-b bandwidths restore size only by
  giving up most of the power. We recommend MZt-GLS with the autoregressive
  estimate, bootstrapped when serial correlation is suspected.
keywords:
  - unit root
  - M tests
  - GLS detrending
  - Monte Carlo test
  - sieve bootstrap
  - long-run variance
  - fixed-b
